1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,997
A restriction is a promise.

2
00:00:01,997 --> 00:00:06,655
A funder hands you money and says, use this for one thing only.

3
00:00:06,655 --> 00:00:11,092
From then on, you have to answer one question at any moment.

4
00:00:11,092 --> 00:00:16,269
How much of that money is left, and can you prove where the rest went?

5
00:00:16,269 --> 00:00:19,375
A normal activity report shows what moved.

6
00:00:19,375 --> 00:00:21,150
It does not answer that.

7
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:25,661
Today I'll show you how to track restricted funds so you can.

8
00:00:25,661 --> 00:00:28,323
Quick note on where I'm coming from.

9
00:00:28,323 --> 00:00:30,837
I build grant compliance software.

10
00:00:30,837 --> 00:00:36,975
To make a computer follow these rules, I had to learn exactly how this money moves.

11
00:00:36,975 --> 00:00:40,376
I'm not an auditor, and I won't talk like one.

12
00:00:40,376 --> 00:00:43,650
You're getting the version a busy director needs.

13
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:46,632
So, what does restricted actually mean?

14
00:00:46,632 --> 00:00:51,986
It means a donor or grantor tied the money to a purpose, or to a time.

15
00:00:51,986 --> 00:00:54,280
Only they can lift that limit.

16
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:57,874
Nonprofit books now sort money into two groups.

17
00:00:57,874 --> 00:01:00,703
With donor restrictions, and without.

18
00:01:00,703 --> 00:01:03,074
Promised money, and free money.

19
00:01:03,074 --> 00:01:07,127
If an old guide lists three groups, it's out of date.

20
00:01:07,127 --> 00:01:08,963
The rule changed to two.

21
00:01:08,963 --> 00:01:10,263
One trap to skip.

22
00:01:10,263 --> 00:01:15,004
Money your board sets aside for a rainy day is not restricted.

23
00:01:15,004 --> 00:01:18,063
The board can change its own mind later.

24
00:01:18,063 --> 00:01:21,460
Only an outside funder can truly restrict a gift.

25
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:23,770
Here's the part people get wrong.

26
00:01:23,770 --> 00:01:28,390
To track a restriction, you need a running balance for each award.

27
00:01:28,390 --> 00:01:30,140
You start with the award.

28
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:32,590
You add anything new that comes in.

29
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:35,600
You subtract what you spend on the purpose.

30
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:42,250
Accountants call that a release, but it just means the promise is being kept, dollar by dollar.

31
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:44,840
You end with a number you can defend.

32
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:51,140
One big checking account and a memo in your books won't give you that per-promise balance.

33
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:54,150
That's the gap, and it's where audits get tense.

34
00:01:54,150 --> 00:01:58,066
Let me show you one way to do this, in the real app.

35
00:01:58,066 --> 00:01:59,798
This is the funds list.

36
00:01:59,798 --> 00:02:03,035
Each fund is one promise, with one purpose.

37
00:02:03,035 --> 00:02:07,779
To make one, you add a fund, give it a name, and pick its type.

38
00:02:07,779 --> 00:02:12,599
Unrestricted, temporarily restricted, or permanently restricted.

39
00:02:12,599 --> 00:02:16,589
Both restricted types are promised money from before.

40
00:02:16,589 --> 00:02:18,924
Unrestricted is the free money.

41
00:02:18,924 --> 00:02:23,920
You can filter the list by type, so the promised money is easy to find.

42
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:27,283
Open a fund, and there's half the answer.

43
00:02:27,283 --> 00:02:28,432
Three numbers.

44
00:02:28,432 --> 00:02:31,385
Allocated, what came into this fund.

45
00:02:31,385 --> 00:02:33,436
Spent, what has gone out.

46
00:02:33,436 --> 00:02:35,158
Balance, what's left.

47
00:02:35,158 --> 00:02:37,701
You don't type that balance in.

48
00:02:37,701 --> 00:02:45,084
The app works it out from the grants feeding the fund and the spending charged against it.

49
00:02:45,084 --> 00:02:52,139
Underneath, you can see exactly which grants fund this, and every expense it paid for.

50
00:02:52,139 --> 00:02:54,250
That's how much is left, live.

51
00:02:54,250 --> 00:02:55,569
Now the other half.

52
00:02:55,569 --> 00:02:56,749
Can you prove it?

53
00:02:56,749 --> 00:03:02,301
Open the Restrictions tab, and there's the same running balance we drew earlier.

54
00:03:02,301 --> 00:03:06,813
Beginning, additions, releases, ending, for the restricted award.

55
00:03:06,813 --> 00:03:10,908
Below it, the app lists each restriction and what it's for.

56
00:03:10,908 --> 00:03:12,921
And it watches your evidence.

57
00:03:12,921 --> 00:03:18,265
If you release money and nothing backs it up, the panel flags it right there.

58
00:03:18,265 --> 00:03:21,666
So you find the weak spot before an auditor does.

59
00:03:21,666 --> 00:03:27,149
This tracking comes with the GrantPipe plans that include restriction lifecycle

60
00:03:27,149 --> 00:03:29,020
so check that it's on your plan.

61
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:29,864
So that's it.

62
00:03:29,864 --> 00:03:33,110
Tracking restricted funds comes down to one thing.

63
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:35,901
A balance you can defend for every promise.

64
00:03:35,901 --> 00:03:41,743
Start it, add to it, release as you spend, and end on a number with the evidence attached.

65
00:03:41,743 --> 00:03:44,859
A spreadsheet can carry this while you're small.

66
00:03:44,859 --> 00:03:47,715
A system carries it for you when you're not.

67
00:03:47,715 --> 00:03:51,221
That's the kind of thing we built GrantPipe to handle.

68
00:03:51,221 --> 00:03:54,207
But the discipline matters more than any tool.

69
00:03:54,207 --> 00:03:59,530
If you want a starting point, we made a free Restricted Fund Tracking Spreadsheet.

70
00:03:59,530 --> 00:04:01,542
It sets up the columns for you.

71
00:04:01,542 --> 00:04:03,360
We'll send it to your inbox.

72
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:07,060
Thanks for watching, and good luck keeping your promises.
